# Stage 5 – Machine Learning with MLflow (AI Use Cases)

---

## 1. Purpose of the Machine Learning Stage

The Machine Learning stage focuses on **transforming cleaned and business-ready data into predictive and strategic insights**.

The objective of this stage is to:
- Apply AI models on Gold-level data
- Generate insights that cannot be achieved using rule-based logic
- Track experiments, models, and results using MLflow
- Integrate AI outputs back into the data platform

This stage demonstrates **thoughtful application of AI**, not just model training.

---

## 2. Why ML Is Applied on Gold Tables

All machine learning models in this project use **Gold-layer Delta tables** as their data source.

This is done because:
- Gold tables are clean, standardized, and business-ready
- Revenue values are already converted to USD
- Calculated columns eliminate the need for repeated preprocessing
- Analytics and ML use the same trusted source of truth

This ensures **consistency between dashboards and AI outputs**.

---

## 3. MLflow Overview

MLflow is used in this project to:
- Track machine learning experiments
- Log model parameters and results
- Compare different runs
- Ensure reproducibility of models

Each AI use case is tracked as a separate MLflow experiment.

---

## 4. AI Use Case 1 – Revenue Prediction (Regression)

### 4.1 Problem Definition

The first AI use case focuses on **predicting total subscription revenue in USD**.

This is a **regression problem** because:
- The output is a continuous numeric value
- Revenue varies across subscriptions and customers

### Business Objective:
- Improve revenue forecasting
- Support pricing and financial planning
- Reduce reliance on manual estimates

---

### 4.2 Input Features

The model uses the following features extracted from the Gold fact table:
- One-off revenue (USD)
- Recurring revenue (USD)
- Contract-related revenue values

These features represent the primary drivers of telecom subscription revenue.

---

### 4.3 Model Selection and Reasoning

A **Linear Regression model** is used as a baseline model.

Reasons for choosing Linear Regression:
- Simple and interpretable
- Easy to explain to business stakeholders
- Provides a strong reference point for performance
- Suitable when transparency is preferred over black-box accuracy

The goal is to **understand revenue behavior**, not maximize complexity.

---

### 4.4 Training and Evaluation

- The dataset is split into training and test sets
- The model is trained on historical data
- Predictions are generated on test data
- Performance is evaluated by comparing actual vs predicted revenue

Visualization is used to assess:
- Alignment between actual and predicted values
- Generalization of the model

---

### 4.5 MLflow Tracking

MLflow tracks:
- Model type and parameters
- Training runs
- Evaluation results
- Model artifacts

This enables:
- Experiment comparison
- Reproducibility
- Transparent model evaluation

---

### 4.6 Output and Business Interpretation

The output of this use case is:
- Predicted total revenue for subscriptions

Business interpretation:
- Close alignment between actual and predicted values indicates reliable forecasting
- Predictions can be used to validate pricing strategies and future revenue expectations

---

### 4.7 Limitations

- Linear Regression assumes linear relationships
- It may not capture complex non-linear revenue patterns

Despite this, it provides a **clear and interpretable baseline** suitable for business use.

---

## 5. AI Use Case 2 – Customer Segmentation (Clustering)

---

### 5.1 Problem Definition

The second AI use case focuses on **segmenting subscriptions based on revenue behavior**.

This is an **unsupervised learning problem** because:
- There are no predefined labels for customer value
- The goal is to discover natural groupings in the data

---

### 5.2 Input Features

The clustering model uses:
- One-off revenue (USD)
- Recurring revenue (USD)
- Total revenue (USD)

These features capture the financial behavior of subscriptions.

---

### 5.3 Model Selection and Reasoning

**K-Means clustering** is selected because:
- It is simple and scalable
- Works well with numerical data
- Produces clear and interpretable clusters

The number of clusters is set to **three** to represent:
- Low-value subscriptions
- Medium-value subscriptions
- High-value subscriptions

---

### 5.4 Training and Evaluation

- The model groups subscriptions based on revenue characteristics
- Cluster quality is evaluated visually by observing separation between clusters
- Clear separation indicates meaningful segmentation

The focus is on **interpretability and actionability**, not mathematical optimization.

---

### 5.5 Output and Business Interpretation

The output of clustering includes:
- A cluster label assigned to each subscription

Business interpretation:
- High-value clusters represent enterprise customers with large or stable revenue
- Medium-value clusters indicate upsell opportunities
- Low-value clusters may require cost optimization or different pricing strategies

---

### 5.6 Business Value of Clustering

Customer segmentation enables:
- Targeted retention strategies
- Differentiated pricing models
- Prioritization of sales and account management efforts

This moves the solution beyond analysis into **decision support**.

---

### 5.7 Limitations

- K-Means requires the number of clusters to be predefined
- Assumes clusters are spherical

These limitations are acceptable given the simplicity and clarity required for business use.

---

## 6. Storing ML Outputs Back to the Platform

The outputs of both ML use cases are:
- Written back to Delta tables
- Made available for dashboards and reporting
- Reusable for future analysis and model retraining

This ensures:
- End-to-end integration between data engineering and AI
- Reusability of AI results
- Transparency in decision-making

---

## 7. Summary

This stage demonstrates how AI is applied thoughtfully using:
- Clean and standardized Gold data
- Interpretable baseline models
- MLflow for experiment tracking
- Clear linkage between predictions and business decisions

The machine learning layer enhances the platform by providing **predictive and strategic insights**, not just descriptive analytics.

---
